<html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Feelings, loneliness and connection in two interviews</title><!--[if gte mso 9]><xml><w:WordDocument><w:View>Print</w:View></w:WordDocument></xml><![endif]--><style>body,p,li,td{font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.35;color:#17130f}h1,h2,h3{font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-weight:normal;color:#8c1912}h1{font-size:20pt}h2{font-size:14pt}p.note{font-size:9pt}td,th{border:1px solid #e4dcd0;padding:3pt 5pt;font-size:9pt;vertical-align:top}table{border-collapse:collapse}</style></head><body><p style="color:#8b7f72;font-size:9pt">RUBICON REPORT</p><h1>Feelings, loneliness and connection in two interviews</h1><p class="asked"><b>Question</b> In interviews I10 (woman, 24, Hackney) and I38 (woman, 22, Tower Hamlets), where does the interviewee express or report their own positive, negative or mixed feelings? For each example: its valence, a short label for the emotion, what it is about, and whether the interviewee ties it in that passage to loneliness, to feeling connected, or to neither; and how many examples of each valence, and of each link, come from each person. Base: the two people, set side by side, with no wider comparison. Feelings the interviewer suggests and the interviewee does not take up, feelings attributed to other people, and purely hypothetical feelings do not count.</p><p>Both women report many feelings of their own, mostly unpleasant ones: of I10&#x27;s, 17 passages are negative, 12 passages positive and 1 passages mixed; of I38&#x27;s, 21 passages are negative, 5 passages positive and 4 passages mixed. They differ most in what they tie their feelings to. I38 ties 16 passages to loneliness, most of them moments of not fitting in: at a new job, at university, among friends who laugh at her or who live differently. I10 ties 8 passages to loneliness, about shallow friendships, working alone and a time when no one helped her; most of her feelings (18 passages) she ties to neither, chiefly fear, hopelessness and anger around an abusive relationship and being refused help, and guilt about family she has lost touch with. I38 ties 10 passages to neither. Each ties a few feelings to connection: I10 4 passages, I38 4 passages. The contrast in loneliness is clear between these two accounts and would hold if several borderline passages were read the other way; it is a contrast between two people and says nothing about anyone else.</p><p>This grid shows how many of each woman&#x27;s feelings are positive, negative or mixed.</p>

<p>This grid shows how many she ties to loneliness, to connection, or to neither.</p>

<h2 id="s-i10-fear-anger-and-guilt-loneliness-about-friendships-and-working-alone">I10: fear, anger and guilt; loneliness about friendships and working alone</h2>
<p>I10 ties loneliness to three things: shallow friendships, working alone and an abusive relationship. She says passing party friendships &quot;can allow you to feel lonely&quot; <sup>1</sup>, and that focusing on too many new people brings stress, &quot;upset and distress&quot;, and is &quot;where the loneliness comes from&quot; <sup>2</sup>, though she puts this in general terms rather than of herself. Working alone as a freelancer gives the most passages. She loves working for herself but misses &quot;interacting with other people that have a similar skill set as me&quot; <sup>3</sup>; lacking chances to build her skills &quot;makes me feel like alone from a work sense&quot; <sup>4</sup>; and after six months on her own she &quot;started to feel really lonely, working alone in the studio&quot; <sup>5</sup>. Two of these passages are positive: she is &quot;much less anxious than I was when I work within a team&quot; <sup>6</sup>, and asked how the loneliness in the studio makes her feel, she answers &quot;Fine, it&#x27;s not like, it&#x27;s not awful&quot; <sup>7</sup>. And when she was in an abusive relationship and kept being refused therapy, she &quot;felt really lonely then&quot; <sup>8</sup>.</p>
<p>That episode carries the feelings she holds most strongly, none of which she ties to loneliness in the same passage: she felt &quot;completely completely unsafe&quot; with &quot;three panic attacks a day&quot; <sup>9</sup>, shock and hopelessness at being turned away again and again <sup>10</sup>, and she is still angry: &quot;it just makes me so mad&quot; <sup>11</sup>. She says she is now &quot;pretty content with it&quot; <sup>12</sup>, and, in the same answer as the anger, that she is content with her drug and alcohol use <sup>13</sup>.</p>
<p>Distance from family brings guilt and anxiety about her father&#x27;s side, &quot;I just don’t even know how to pick up the phone&quot; <sup>14</sup>, guilt at not supporting her mother <sup>15</sup>, and sadness that &quot;I missed out&quot; <sup>16</sup>. Friendships that faded leave a hurt: it &quot;kind of hurts that some people aren’t still in your life&quot; <sup>17</sup>. Her feelings about other people&#x27;s conduct she also ties to neither: she finds it horrible that people care mainly about themselves <sup>18</sup>, selfishness &quot;makes me feel sad&quot; <sup>19</sup>, the race for followers makes her angry rather than sad <sup>20</sup>, she feels sorry for those who promote themselves that way <sup>21</sup>, she is grateful not to be affected by pressure on body image <sup>22</sup>, and her best friend&#x27;s leaving social media over it &quot;breaks my heart&quot; <sup>23</sup>.</p>
<p>Her sense of connection rests on a &quot;really really really stable group of friends&quot; whom she loves and trusts <sup>24</sup>, on the excitement of meeting new people <sup>25</sup>, on being approached by other creatives, &quot;that’s really nice&quot; <sup>26</sup>, and on local bars where she feels &quot;most socially connected&quot; <sup>27</sup>. Her other positive feelings are feeling &quot;stronger being able to kind of reject&quot; passing friendships <sup>28</sup>, being glad she invested in her career <sup>29</sup>, and loving a local bar&#x27;s green approach <sup>30</sup>.</p>
<h2 id="s-i38-loneliness-as-not-fitting-in-connection-as-acceptance">I38: loneliness as not fitting in, connection as acceptance</h2>
<p>Most of I38&#x27;s loneliness comes from feeling she does not fit. Among family and friends who talk about football, &quot;I can’t socially connect with them&quot; <sup>31</sup>. Comparing her two hundred followers with others&#x27; thousands makes her wonder what she is doing wrong, and she ends &quot;you still feel lonely&quot; <sup>32</sup>. Starting her first graduate job she &quot;felt a bit lonely&quot; <sup>33</sup> and felt &quot;like an outsider&quot; <sup>34</sup>; at first she felt &quot;a bit distressed and a bit of anxiety&quot; and &quot;genuinely felt very lonely&quot; <sup>35</sup>; she envied friends who had made friends at work when &quot;I don’t even have anyone to have lunch with&quot; <sup>36</sup>; and she &quot;used to go home really upset and lonely&quot; and still feels &quot;a bit frustrated&quot; <sup>37</sup>. A slow job search brought &quot;a sense of loneliness&quot; with feeling &quot;I was incompetent&quot; <sup>38</sup>. At university, as a Muslim among people who drank and went clubbing, she &quot;felt really lonely&quot; <sup>39</sup> and &quot;a bit of an outcast&quot;, though that eased and &quot;I don’t mind, it’s okay&quot; <sup>40</sup>. When friends laugh at her anxiety she feels &quot;like an odd black sheep in the group&quot; <sup>41</sup>; she struggles to open up about personal issues because &quot;they would judge me&quot; <sup>42</sup>; and she felt friends &quot;still look at me weirdly&quot; for leaving to pray <sup>43</sup>, though in the same answer she says they have since come to respect her religion.</p>
<p>Three of her loneliness passages are not about fitting in: seeing others out when she has &quot;no one to go out with&quot; <sup>44</sup>, enjoying her own company although &quot;it gets a bit boring&quot; <sup>45</sup>, and no longer minding eating lunch on her own <sup>46</sup>.</p>
<p>The feelings she ties to neither are mostly about work and comparison: missing her university freedom <sup>47</sup>, feeling &quot;so robotic when I went to work&quot; <sup>48</sup>, &quot;it’s just that constant fear&quot; of getting things wrong <sup>49</sup>, being &quot;so fed up with this nonsense&quot; at having to accept it <sup>50</sup>, upset at earning less than her friends <sup>51</sup>, friends&#x27; posts about lunches at work, which &quot;made me feel really bad&quot;, though she is glad she later spoke to them <sup>52</sup>, a sense that others&#x27; luxury lives are unfair <sup>53</sup>, dislike of drunken clubbing <sup>54</sup>, and anxiety about her morning routine <sup>55</sup>. Influencers&#x27; wealth affected her when she was younger, but now &quot;I’m content and I feel happier&quot; <sup>56</sup>.</p>
<p>Connection, for her, is acceptance without judgement: she feels &quot;socially accepted in these places&quot; at the gym and pool <sup>57</sup>, &quot;a sense of belongingness&quot; at the mosque in Ramadan <sup>58</sup>, &quot;I feel socially connected&quot; at meals with friends and family <sup>59</sup>, and understood by someone who listened rather than laughed <sup>60</sup>.</p>
<h2 id="s-the-two-side-by-side">The two side by side</h2>
<p>For I38 loneliness is mainly about not fitting in with the people around her, and connection, found where she is not judged, is its remedy. (see “I38: loneliness as not fitting in, connection as acceptance”) I10 ties fewer feelings to loneliness, mostly about shallow friendships and working alone, and two of those are positive feelings about working alone; her strongest feelings concern an abusive relationship and the services that refused her, and many others concern family and other people&#x27;s conduct. (see “I10: fear, anger and guilt; loneliness about friendships and working alone”) Her connection rests chiefly on a long-standing group of close friends. I10 also reports more positive feelings than I38 (12 passages against 5 passages); I am fairly sure of this difference too, though less so than of the difference in loneliness, since it rests on how passages are divided.</p>
<h2 id="s-how-far-this-can-be-relied-on">How far this can be relied on</h2>
<p>The counts depend on where one passage is judged to end and the next to begin, since a feeling repeated about the same thing is counted once; they show where each woman&#x27;s feelings lie, not exact totals. A link counts only where the speaker makes it in the passage, and a feeling about being alone counts as tied to loneliness whatever its valence, which is why two of I10&#x27;s positive feelings about working alone are among her loneliness passages. Four passages are weak: I10&#x27;s stress at too many new people <sup>2</sup> and I38&#x27;s loneliness over followers <sup>32</sup> are put in general terms, and I38&#x27;s difficulty opening up <sup>42</sup> and going home upset <sup>37</sup> largely restate other passages. Feelings attributed to others are not counted: I10 says people who chase followers &quot;must feel lonely&quot; (I10), but that is about them, not her.</p><h2>How this was made</h2><h3>What was read</h3><p>All 48 documents, read in full: 24 male, 24 female.</p>
<h3>Coding: Emotions</h3><p class="small">one row for each passage that fits; 60 rows.</p><details><summary>The instruction a second coder would follow</summary><blockquote>Read the interview. Find every passage in which the interviewee (not the interviewer) expresses or reports a feeling of their own: a feeling they have now or have had, in their own words. Loneliness counts as a feeling. A generalised &#x27;you&#x27; or &#x27;it can feel&#x27; counts only where the interviewee ties it in that passage to their own experience. Do not code: feelings the interviewee imagines for a figure in a drawing or for people in general; purely hypothetical feelings (&#x27;I would feel&#x27;); feelings the interviewer puts forward that the interviewee only agrees to with &#x27;yeah&#x27; or &#x27;uhum&#x27;. Where the same feeling about the same thing is repeated or elaborated in follow-up answers, code it once, quoting the clearest words. Where one answer names feelings about two different things, code each. Where one answer names several feelings about the same thing, code them as one passage and name them all in the emotion label; where a later answer names a different feeling about the same thing, code that too. Code a feeling the interviewee has about another person&#x27;s situation (for example, sadness at a friend&#x27;s distress) as their own feeling, but not a feeling they say the other person has. A reported absence of feeling (&#x27;it doesn&#x27;t make me feel anything&#x27;) is not coded. For each passage, copy the words exactly and fill the columns.</blockquote></details>
<h4>Valence</h4><p>Whether the feeling expressed is pleasant, unpleasant or both.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Positive</th><td>A pleasant feeling: happiness, enjoyment, relief, gratitude, love, hope, ease, respect, calm acceptance.</td><td>Statements such as &#x27;I feel good, happy&#x27;, &#x27;I enjoy&#x27;, &#x27;it&#x27;s alright now&#x27;.</td><td>Neutral description of an activity with no feeling named or plainly expressed.</td></tr><tr><th>Negative</th><td>An unpleasant feeling: sadness, loneliness, frustration, resentment, boredom, unease, longing, resignation, distress.</td><td>Statements such as &#x27;it makes me sad&#x27;, &#x27;I feel quite lonely&#x27;, &#x27;I don&#x27;t feel okay&#x27;.</td><td>A passage that pairs an unpleasant feeling with a pleasant or accepting one about the same thing: code mixed.</td></tr><tr><th>Mixed</th><td>Pleasant and unpleasant feelings, or acceptance and hurt, expressed together about the same thing.</td><td>Statements such as &#x27;conflicting emotions&#x27; or &#x27;I&#x27;m used to it now but sometimes it can get to me&#x27;. Also an unpleasant feeling in the past and a pleasant or accepting one now about the same thing, in the same passage (&#x27;when I was a lot younger, it did affect me ... I’m content and I feel happier&#x27;).</td><td>Two feelings about different things in the same answer: code each separately.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h4>Emotion</h4><p>A short label, one to three words, for the feeling, such as loneliness, frustration, gratitude, relief; where one passage names several feelings, list each.</p>
<h4>Subject</h4><p>In a few words, what the feeling is about or what brings it on, as the interviewee describes it.</p>
<h4>Link</h4><p>Whether the interviewee, in the coded passage itself, ties the feeling to loneliness or to feeling connected.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Loneliness</th><td>The feeling is loneliness, or the interviewee ties it in the passage to loneliness, being alone, having no one, not fitting in, or being an outsider or &#x27;black sheep&#x27;.</td><td>&#x27;that sometimes makes me feel lonely&#x27;; &#x27;why do I feel like an outsider&#x27;; &#x27;I don&#x27;t mind if I don&#x27;t sit with anyone&#x27; (a feeling about being alone, whatever its valence). A feeling about being or working alone counts whatever its valence, including relief or enjoyment at working alone rather than with others (&#x27;much less anxious than I was when I work within a team&#x27;).</td><td>A feeling about a situation the interviewee elsewhere calls lonely, where this passage does not make the tie; loneliness the interviewee attributes to other people. Enjoyment of freedom or independence where the passage does not speak of being alone or without other people (code neither).</td></tr><tr><th>Connection</th><td>The interviewee ties the feeling in the passage to feeling connected, belonging, being accepted or understood, or closeness and enjoyment with other people.</td><td>&#x27;a sense of belongingness&#x27;; &#x27;I felt like they understood me&#x27;; &#x27;I love them and trust them with everything&#x27;.</td><td>Enjoyment of a place, activity or freedom with no other people named as the source of the feeling.</td></tr><tr><th>Neither</th><td>The passage ties the feeling to neither loneliness nor connection.</td><td>Anger at a service, guilt about family contact, anxiety about a routine, where the passage names no tie.</td><td>Any passage that fits loneliness or connection.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>What was counted</h3><ul><li>Rows by document and valence <span class="small">(by_valence)</span></li><li>Rows by document and link <span class="small">(by_link)</span></li></ul>
<h3>The checks code ran</h3><ul><li>60 of 60 quotations found word for word in their document.</li><li>14 numbers in the answer, every one counted by code; 0 written by hand.</li><li>0 citations to passages that do not exist; 0 quotations not in the passage they cite.</li></ul>
<h3>The second reading</h3><p>A second coder coded the documents afresh to the same definitions without seeing the first coding. A fresh reader then ruled on every place the two differed, and checked every sentence and quotation of the answer against the documents, correcting the coding and the answer where they were wrong. Its record is <code>check.md</code>, in the run's zip with the second coder's rows.</p><p class="small">- Disagreements between the coders, ruled passage by passage: 19. Ruled for the rows (first coder): 8. Ruled for the second coder: 11. Left unsettled: 0 (two were settled by tightening definitions in workflow.json, below).
- Rows changed: 19 in all (11 edited, 3 removed by merging, 5 added); 58 rows became 60.
- Definitions tightened: 2 (link &quot;loneliness&quot;; valence &quot;mixed&quot;).
- Sentences in answer.md: 32 of the 40 original sentences changed or cut, 8 kept; the report now has 48 sentences and cites every row.
- Recount: clean (no missing citations, no bare numbers, no quotations not found or not in what they cite).</p>
<h3>The record</h3><p>Code drew this report from the run's zip, in-interviews-i10-woman-24-hackney-and-i38-woman-22-2026-10-08-6f101b2a.zip, with Rubicon 0.1.0+2a4518694e68. The zip's fingerprint is <code class="fingerprint">6f101b2af417c204932b0b113ca40e07f0bec2b55b3982010434510b8281d138</code>. It is the SHA-256 of the zip's SHA256SUMS file, which lists the SHA-256 of every other file in the zip, so changing any file breaks the match. To check that the zip is the one this report was drawn from, unzip it, run <code>sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS</code>, then <code>sha256sum SHA256SUMS</code>, and compare the result with the fingerprint above. Rubicon's own code, which is public, checks the rest: <code>python verify.py &lt;report&gt; &lt;zip&gt;</code> recounts every number from the coded passages in the zip, draws the report again from the zip and says whether it matches this one exactly.</p><h2>Passages cited</h2><p class="note"><sup>1</sup> I10: "that can allow you to feel lonely. Yes, so that’s something I’ve experienced a lot here"</p><p class="note"><sup>2</sup> I10: "it becomes stressful in an already stressful life style, you know, it’s kind of you are adding extra stress to your plate for something that probably doesn’t matter that much, and that’s what causes, you know, upset and distress and that can be lots of other problems, you know, in making new friends, you know, I have a lot of very big group of friends and they are all amazing but it’s almost like if somebody… when new people trying enter your scene, or you trying to enter someone else’s scene, that can also cause problems within the dynamics of the other people when I don’t want to be a part of them when I already have my… you know. Yes, so I think really by focusing on too many other people, making too many friends, you really are only harming yourself. And it’s sad coz then you…you haven’t put as much effort into that people that you really care about and that’s where the loneliness comes from."</p><p class="note"><sup>3</sup> I10: "definitely in the summer, it was very difficult for me. You know, I love working for myself, and a lot of people do it now, it’s like very very very common for freelancers, especially in creative fields. And it has like lots of benefits, you know, I can wake up when I want, I can do what I want, I can set my own schedules, but I think what we kind of miss, well, what I certainly miss is interacting with other people that have a similar skill set as me. I think it’s a real shame"</p><p class="note"><sup>4</sup> I10: "lacking in being able to go and expand your skills, makes me feel like alone from a work sense"</p><p class="note"><sup>5</sup> I10: "I started to feel really lonely, working alone in the studio"</p><p class="note"><sup>6</sup> I10: "I’m also much less anxious than I was when I work within a team"</p><p class="note"><sup>7</sup> I10: "Fine, it&#x27;s not like, it&#x27;s not awful and I really, like I love to be able to just get up when I want and just do whatever I want"</p><p class="note"><sup>8</sup> I10: "Say I felt really lonely then, at that point of moment I’ve, you know, under somebody could have made me not feel alone but they didn’t"</p><p class="note"><sup>9</sup> I10: "I felt completely completely unsafe. I was having three panic attacks a day"</p><p class="note"><sup>10</sup> I10: "to continually be turned away really makes you feel like &quot;I’m fucked! I’m never gonna get out of this”"</p><p class="note"><sup>11</sup> I10: "it just makes me so mad because I feel like, if they have given me some help at that point, that my situation might not have gone to the point that it did"</p><p class="note"><sup>12</sup> I10: "That’s OK, you know, I’m pretty content with it now."</p><p class="note"><sup>13</sup> I10: "I’m pretty content with it now, you know, spend a long time like partying"</p><p class="note"><sup>14</sup> I10: "I feel so guilty for not speaking to these people for such a long time. And I could have tried harder. I’m sure but there’s so many other things that I have to focus on and now I’m not [1:01:36]upon… but I’m just like, I just don’t even know how to do it, like I just don’t even know how to pick up the phone, because I’m that anxious that they are, like upset with me"</p><p class="note"><sup>15</sup> I10: "my mom’s been through lots of things since I’ve been here and I feel guilty for that as well"</p><p class="note"><sup>16</sup> I10: "I just feel it makes me like, feel sad for myself. I feel like I missed out."</p><p class="note"><sup>17</sup> I10: "there are things that you know friendships that I look back on now I think that’s like a massive shame when it makes you…you know, kind of hurts that some people aren’t still in your life"</p><p class="note"><sup>18</sup> I10: "the fact that no one cares, I mean it is horrible because I wish that we could all care about each other as much like as we care about ourselves"</p><p class="note"><sup>19</sup> I10: "be focused on what you are doing without, you know, being completely selfish and disregarding everybody else’s feeling. That makes me feel sad."</p><p class="note"><sup>20</sup> I10: "I just think it’s ridiculous. It makes me angry, it doesn’t make me sad, it just make me angry."</p><p class="note"><sup>21</sup> I10: "I feel more sorry for the people that are promoting themselves that way"</p><p class="note"><sup>22</sup> I10: "I feel like really grateful that I’m not that heavily affected by … you know … something that other women are"</p><p class="note"><sup>23</sup> I10: "she said she just can’t face looking at all the beautiful girls. That breaks my heart"</p><p class="note"><sup>24</sup> I10: "I’m more content with it now than I have been, but I think that’s because I now have been here a long time like I have a really really really stable group of friends that, you know, I love them and trust them with everything"</p><p class="note"><sup>25</sup> I10: "it’s very exciting meeting new people, especially we do lots of partying so, you know, just spend amazing times with new people"</p><p class="note"><sup>26</sup> I10: "sometimes I get approached and that’s really nice"</p><p class="note"><sup>27</sup> I10: "places I feel most socially connected, there are three bars in this area"</p><p class="note"><sup>28</sup> I10: "now I know where to focus my energy, it makes me feel stronger being able to kind of reject that"</p><p class="note"><sup>29</sup> I10: "I’m really glad that I put that time into it"</p><p class="note"><sup>30</sup> I10: "completely green, which is really amazing for us in Hackney, I love things like that"</p><p class="note"><sup>31</sup> I38: "I feel as though I can’t socially connect with them"</p><p class="note"><sup>32</sup> I38: "There’s me with just my two hundred followers and then you’ve got someone who’s got over fifty thousand followers, it does make you feel as though, oh you know, how do these people get so many followers and how do they become that, how do they live that lifestyle, how do they just become an instagram fashion model etc, starting off with nothing and having so many big things, so it just makes you feel as though what are you doing wrong in order for you to become like that. So, I just sometimes feel like maybe it does separate people sometimes. It makes people more…it’s not envious or jealousy, but it just separates people a lot more, because how do people portray, for example ‘Oh I’ve got fifty million followers, what do you have’. It’s like saying you’ve got nothing compared to me, so you’re not my friend type of thing. So, something like that. That’s what I mean, you have some people around you, but you still feel lonely."</p><p class="note"><sup>33</sup> I38: "I felt a bit lonely, because I saw everyone there at the work place, they know what they’re doing, everyone has already made their friendship groups"</p><p class="note"><sup>34</sup> I38: "I felt as if why couldn’t I fit in like that, why do I feel like an outsider"</p><p class="note"><sup>35</sup> I38: "I did feel a bit distressed and a bit of anxiety, I felt as though a lot of people, I thought is this just me or does everybody go through this. It made me wonder a little bit and I just gave myself like a period of time where ok I’m just going to be here for another few months, let me see how this goes, as time went on, I slowly adapted and managed that transition phase and I was able to actually fit in well. I just feel as though you just need a bit of time to adapt to change, that’s what I needed and I wasn’t used to that, but at the very beginning I genuinely felt very lonely"</p><p class="note"><sup>36</sup> I38: "slightly envious and jealous like at the fact that how have they fitted in so well and they managed to make a whole bunch of friends when I have been here a couple of months and I don’t even have anyone to have lunch with"</p><p class="note"><sup>37</sup> I38: "I struggled to fit in at work, I used to go home really upset and lonely, I still feel a bit frustrated"</p><p class="note"><sup>38</sup> I38: "I did feel a sense of loneliness in terms of, I felt not dumb…I felt as though I wasn’t capable, I was incompetent"</p><p class="note"><sup>39</sup> I38: "I felt really lonely because obviously I had to make completely new friends when I went to university"</p><p class="note"><sup>40</sup> I38: "maybe a bit of an outcast because my friends are not more slim as I am and it had a huge impact on my social life, but as time has gone on and I’ve managed, social media, I just don’t, it doesn’t really interest me and I don’t mind, it’s okay"</p><p class="note"><sup>41</sup> I38: "it just makes me feel a bit low, like okay they’re just laughing at me and I just feel like an odd black sheep in the group"</p><p class="note"><sup>42</sup> I38: "not having anyone to talk to about your personal issues. This is something that I faced quite a bit when I was currently now and sometimes when I was a lot younger. As personal issues, meaning it can be anything from you feel like something dramatic happened in your life to just about little things, I struggled, I sometimes, although when everyone was around me, I struggled to open up about me, I feel as though they would judge me"</p><p class="note"><sup>43</sup> I38: "it was very difficult for them to understand and I felt as though they still look at me weirdly"</p><p class="note"><sup>44</sup> I38: "I feel lonely, because I feel as though sometimes everyone is busy with their lives and I have no one to go out with"</p><p class="note"><sup>45</sup> I38: "I do enjoy my own company, but it gets a bit boring on your own enjoying your own company"</p><p class="note"><sup>46</sup> I38: "I don’t mind if I don’t if I don’t sit with anyone on my lunch break anymore, I don’t mind if I don’t have anyone to talk to"</p><p class="note"><sup>47</sup> I38: "I missed my social life. I missed the fact that I was able to act, just the freedom"</p><p class="note"><sup>48</sup> I38: "I felt as though I was so robotic when I went to work"</p><p class="note"><sup>49</sup> I38: "it’s just that constant fear and just trying to please people"</p><p class="note"><sup>50</sup> I38: "I hated the fact that I accepted it. It was more like, I just felt more like, ugh I’m just so fed up with this nonsense"</p><p class="note"><sup>51</sup> I38: "I did feel a bit upset, that my friends managed to get a job where they were earning up to 30,000"</p><p class="note"><sup>52</sup> I38: "it made me feel really bad and I’m glad I spoke to my friends"</p><p class="note"><sup>53</sup> I38: "I think to myself it’s not fair, how do people actually have no responsibilities"</p><p class="note"><sup>54</sup> I38: "I hate stuff like that, I’d rather be at home watching tv in my slippers"</p><p class="note"><sup>55</sup> I38: "I struggle from anxiety in the mornings I have a routine and that needs to be done"</p><p class="note"><sup>56</sup> I38: "I feel as though, when I was a lot younger, it did affect me quite a bit. Obviously, I was young, I wasn’t working. I relied on pocket money to get certain things and I see all these massive influencing bloggers with so much money and so many things and what not, I feel as though as I’ve gotten older and I’m working and I’m earning, my responsibilities are a lot more. I realised these are not the things I actually need in life. I’m content and I feel happier knowing the fact that I do have my stuff"</p><p class="note"><sup>57</sup> I38: "I feel socially accepted in these places"</p><p class="note"><sup>58</sup> I38: "it keeps you a sense of happiness and a sense of belongingness"</p><p class="note"><sup>59</sup> I38: "I feel socially connected when I do go out with my friends or family and we do go out for a meal etc. I feel as though it’s a good and happy environment"</p><p class="note"><sup>60</sup> I38: "I felt comfortable talking to them because I felt like they understood me rather than just laughing at me"</p><h2>What next</h2><p><b>For the evaluator:</b> Treat this report as a draft. Before it is final, make sense of it with the people it concerns, such as programme staff and participants: whether the findings ring true, what they leave out, and what to do about them. Rubicon can help discuss how to build their responses back into a revised answer.</p><p><b>For evaluation commissioners:</b> Parallel to this report is a zip file (in-interviews-i10-woman-24-hackney-and-i38-woman-22-2026-10-08-6f101b2a.zip), saved in the same folder, which contains the whole run: the documents, the coded passages and the workflow that recounts every number. Its fingerprint, under How this was made, ties it to this report: change a file in either and they no longer match. With it, anyone can check every number against the coded passages, and code the documents again to the same definitions without Rubicon or any other particular software. A fresh coding will not reproduce this report word for word, because AI models are unpredictable, but it should come close.</p><p>The Rubicon plugin for Claude is provided for free by Causal Map Ltd. Causal Map also runs consultancy services on analysing qualitative evidence for evaluation: <a href="https://causalmap.app/?utm_source=rubicon-plugin&amp;utm_medium=report">causalmap.app</a>. Coming soon: continue your Rubicon work at the Rubicon website.</p></body></html>